! Face-centred-cubic nickel with an ULTRASOFT dataset, collinear: the cell the
! augmentation charge inside a transverse matrix element is measured on.
!
! Every magnon here before P93 was norm-conserving, because
! `<psi_up| e^{-i(q+G)r} |psi_dn>` is the plane-wave overlap of the pseudo
! states only when all of the charge is in `|psi|^2`. With an ultrasoft dataset
! it is not, and what is missing is `Q_ij(q+G)` -- the same augmentation charge
! the ultrasoft Berry phase already carries at an arbitrary wavevector.
!
! The check the cell exists for is the Goldstone identity, `X_0 B_xc = m` at
! `q = 0`, which is a statement about the *whole* moment: a spin wave of
! infinite wavelength is a rigid rotation and costs nothing. Most of a
! transition metal's moment lives in the d shell, which is exactly where an
! ultrasoft dataset puts its augmentation, so the identity fails by roughly the
! augmentation's share of the moment when the term is dropped and holds when it
! is kept. That is a check whose null result cannot be mistaken for a pass.
!
! `nosym` and `noinv` with the whole 4x4x4 grid, for the reason
! `h-fcc-magnon.in` gives: the states at `k + q` are read out of this same grid,
! which needs it closed under translation by `q`, and `X_0(G, G')` is a matrix
! in two G indices that nothing here symmetrises. LDA rather than PBE, so that
! the kernel `B_xc/m` is the local field and nothing else.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 2, celldm(1) = 6.65,
    nat = 1, ntyp = 1,
    ecutwfc = 25.0, ecutrho = 200.0,
    occupations = 'smearing', smearing = 'mv', degauss = 0.02,
    nspin = 2
    nosym = .true., noinv = .true.
    starting_magnetization(1) = 0.5
    nbnd = 14
 /
 &electrons
    mixing_beta = 0.3
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
    electron_maxstep = 200
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Ni 58.6934 Ni.pz-nd-rrkjus.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (crystal)
 Ni 0.0 0.0 0.0
K_POINTS {automatic}
 4 4 4 0 0 0
